= Embodied cognition — Technical =

== Strength of embodiment claims ==
Embodiment claims range from weak to strong:

* bodily states modulate cognition;
* sensorimotor systems contribute representational content;
* cognition depends constitutively on action/environment loops;
* some cognitive processes extend beyond organism boundaries.

These claims require different evidence.

== Grounded representation ==
Barsalou-style perceptual-symbol accounts propose that conceptual processing partially reinstates modality-specific states.

This differs from a strictly amodal symbolic architecture.

== Enactive approaches ==
Enactivist theories emphasize autonomous organism–environment coupling, sense-making, and action.

They can reject assumptions shared by simulation-based grounded-cognition theories.

== Experimental interpretation ==
Motor or perceptual activation during conceptual tasks can show involvement without proving that the activated system is necessary or constitutive.

Lesion, interference, timing, and causal-manipulation evidence provide stronger tests.

== Relation to distributed cognition ==
[[Distributed cognition]] changes the system-level unit of analysis across people and artifacts.

Embodied cognition emphasizes bodily and sensorimotor constitution. The two can overlap but are not synonyms.

